	faithHeading="Faith and the Bible in Poland"
	faithBodyHtml={`<p>In the 2021 national census, <strong>71.3% of Poles</strong> (27.1 million people) were counted as Roman Catholic &mdash; down from about 88% in 2011 &mdash; while 6.8% said they have no religion and a further 20% chose not to answer the question. Among those who did answer, 89.8% said they are Catholic.</p><p>The Bible is woven into that story. Poland has had a complete Bible in its own language since the Brest Bible of 1563, and Catholics and Protestants have each given it a translation of their own &mdash; Wujek (1599), Gdańsk (1632), the Millennium Bible (1965) and the Warsaw Bible (1975).</p><p>Faith in Poland is real and public, yet it is shifting. That is the opening for a Father&rsquo;s heart: not to replace the Church or the Bible Poland treasures, but to hear the Scriptures again, in clear Polish, as the words of a Father who is near.</p>`}
